T_COMP="Maintenance means removing what is no longer needed: useless software, copies of downloads, caches. Why: these leftovers build up silently, take up space on the disk and slow it down."
T_CMD="apt-get -s autoremove, du, dpkg -l, journalctl (DMP uniquement)"
T_O() {
O="🧹 Regular maintenance keeps the computer light, like tidying your house. Here is what builds up, with the way to remove it.${NL}${NL}1) 📦 Packages that have become useless: $AR${NL}   • Role: software installed automatically to serve other software, which nothing uses any more.${NL}   • What to do: sudo apt autoremove (read the proposed list before confirming).${NL}${NL}2) 🗃️ Copies of downloaded packages: $CA MB${NL}   • Role: Debian keeps the installation files just in case.${NL}   • What to do: sudo apt clean (erases these copies, without risk: they are downloaded again if needed).${NL}${NL}3) 🗂️ Cache of your applications: $UC MB${NL}   • Role: files kept to speed up your programs (thumbnails, browser).${NL}   • What to do: they do not count in the assessment of the machine: they rebuild themselves and speed up your programs. Only delete them if you are short of space.${NL}${NL}4) 🗑️ Trash: $TR MB${NL}   • What to do: empty it if you no longer need these files (they can still be recovered as long as it is full).${NL}${NL}5) 🫀 Linux kernels installed: $KE${NL}   • Why: Debian keeps the old kernel as a backup in case the new one causes problems. Two is normal, more than three can be cleaned with autoremove.${NL}${NL}6) 🕳️ Leftover settings of removed software: $RC${NL}   • What to do: sudo apt purge \$(dpkg -l | awk '/^rc/{print \$2}') erases them (very little space gained).${NL}${NL}💽 Space that can be recovered without risk (package copies and trash): about $REC MB"
case $CL in
big) R="A cleanup would be useful"; X="More than 2 GB can be recovered. Start with “sudo apt clean” (safe), then empty the trash. Why: a disk that fills up slows the system down (see page B4).";;
some) R="Cleanup possible"; X="A little space can be recovered: “sudo apt autoremove” and “sudo apt clean” are enough. Nothing urgent.";;
*) R="Clean system"; X="There is very little waste: nothing to do. Check again after a big update.";;
esac
}
